Delayed controller onboarding checks
===================================

The automatic connection check created during controller onboarding is bound to
that controller's original site and organization. It requires an active,
nondeleted controller, site and organization before it reads credentials and
contacts the device. A reassigned controller is not contacted by this old check.

The database session closes before adapter connection and test I/O. On completion,
a new session checks current ownership and compares the connection settings,
configuration, row update timestamp and stored outcome with the original snapshot.
If anything changed, the old result is discarded. Current controller, site and
organization rows are locked through the outcome commit. No authority row lock is
held during device I/O. Credential decryption errors now enter the same safe error
bookkeeping as adapter failures, without exposing the credential or exception body.

A configuration edit before execution is allowed in the original active scope;
that current configuration is tested. An edit during I/O requires a new check.
Metadata edits that advance the row timestamp also discard the delayed outcome.

Limits
------
Creation-time user permissions are not revalidated at execution. This change does
not cancel a network request already in flight if authority changes after the
initial check, and it is not distributed operation fencing. Discovery publication
still occurs separately after committing a successful outcome: broker outages and
the postcommit authorization race require separate recovery/worker controls. This
is not a durable job outbox, exactly-once execution, or live appliance certification.
Deploy every API process; old processes retain the previous delayed-check behavior.
